|~ What is declared under `::vec`: the refusal of its constructor, and
   the verbs of vectors, each with the parts of its args and its
   answer; a quote reads as the vector of its steps. Each verb is a name with the parts of its args, read and held
   to their laws before the primitive runs. ~|

:vec/construct
  |~~ Reads a vector as the bare vector, and a set, a quote or a doc as the vector of its elements. ~~|
  ::builtin{}

:vec/PayloadNotVecError
  |~~ `::vec` reads a vector, a set or a quote as the bare vector; a
      payload of another kind is refused.
  ~~|

:vec/PayloadNotVecError/refusesAMap
  |~~ A map payload is refused. ~~|
  ::law~(::vec{:a 1} !| [type /actualType] | eq [::vec/PayloadNotVecError ::map])

:vec/count
  |~~ The number of elements. ~~|
  ::builtin{}

:vec/count/countsTheElements
  |~~ Three elements count three. ~~|
  ::law~([1 2 3] | count | eq 3)

:vec/count/size
  |~~ How many elements the vector holds. ~~|
  ::number

:vec/empty
  |~~ Whether the vector holds no element. ~~|
  ::builtin{}

:vec/empty/holdsOfTheEmptyVector
  |~~ The empty vector is empty. ~~|
  ::law~([] | empty | eq true)

:vec/empty/failsOfAnElement
  |~~ A vector of one element is not empty. ~~|
  ::law~([1] | empty | eq false)

:vec/empty/vacant
  |~~ Whether the vector holds no element. ~~|
  ::boolean

:vec/first
  |~~ The first element, or null for the empty vector. ~~|
  ::builtin{}

:vec/first/answersTheFirst
  |~~ The first element answers. ~~|
  ::law~([10 20 30] | first | eq 10)

:vec/first/answersNullOfNothing
  |~~ The empty vector has no first element. ~~|
  ::law~([] | first | eq null)

:vec/last
  |~~ The last element, or null for the empty vector. ~~|
  ::builtin{}

:vec/last/answersTheLast
  |~~ The last element answers. ~~|
  ::law~([10 20 30] | last | eq 30)

:vec/last/answersNullOfNothing
  |~~ The empty vector has no last element. ~~|
  ::law~([] | last | eq null)

:vec/sum
  |~~ The sum of the elements, each a number; the empty vector sums to 0. ~~|
  ::builtin{}

:vec/sum/addsTheElements
  |~~ The sum adds every element. ~~|
  ::law~([1 2 3 4] | sum | eq 10)

:vec/sum/sumsNothingToZero
  |~~ The empty vector sums to zero. ~~|
  ::law~([] | sum | eq 0)

:vec/sum/total
  |~~ The sum of the elements. ~~|
  ::number

:vec/sum/ElementNotNumberError
  |~~ `sum` adds numbers; `:index` names the first element that is
      none, and a field is read first, `* /amount | sum`.
  ~~|

:vec/sum/ElementNotNumberError/refusesAString
  |~~ A string among the numbers is refused at its index. ~~|
  ::law~([1 "two" 3] | sum !| [type /actualType /index] | eq [::vec/sum/ElementNotNumberError ::string 1])

:vec/avg
  |~~ The mean of the elements, each a number, or null when there is none. ~~|
  ::builtin{}

:vec/avg/answersTheMean
  |~~ The mean of one to four is two and a half. ~~|
  ::law~([1 2 3 4] | avg | eq 2.5)

:vec/avg/answersNullOfNothing
  |~~ The empty vector has no mean. ~~|
  ::law~([] | avg | eq null)

:vec/avg/averagesEachGroup
  |~~ The mean of each group comes under its key. ~~|
  ::law~([{:d :a :n 1} {:d :a :n 3} {:d :b :n 5}] | groupBy ~(/d) * (* /n | avg) | eq {:a 2 :b 5})

:vec/avg/mean
  |~~ The mean of the elements, null for an empty vector. ~~|
  #[::number ::null]

:vec/avg/ElementNotNumberError
  |~~ `avg` takes the mean of numbers; `:index` names the first element
      that is none, and a field is read first, `* /amount | avg`.
  ~~|

:vec/avg/ElementNotNumberError/refusesAString
  |~~ A string among the numbers is refused at its index. ~~|
  ::law~([1 "two" 3] | avg !| [type /actualType /index] | eq [::vec/avg/ElementNotNumberError ::string 1])

:vec/min
  |~~ The first element in the one order of values, or the one whose key
      answers the first value, the key read as `sort` reads it. The
      element itself is the answer, the first of equal ones, and the
      empty vector yields null.
  ~~|
  ::builtin{}

:vec/min/answersTheLeast
  |~~ The least element answers. ~~|
  ::law~([3 1 4 1 5] | min | eq 1)

:vec/min/answersTheElementOfTheLeastKey
  |~~ The element whose key answers least answers whole. ~~|
  ::law~([{:age 30} {:age 20}] | min ~(/age) | eq {:age 20})

:vec/min/ordersAcrossKinds
  |~~ Null precedes every other kind in the one order. ~~|
  ::law~([3 "a" null] | min | eq null)

:vec/min/key
  |~~ The key the call gives, null when it gives none. ~~|
  ~(at 0)

:vec/min/key/NotQuoteOrNullError
  |~~ The key is code, a quote or null. ~~|
  ::holds~(is ::quote ::null)

:vec/max
  |~~ The last element in the one order of values, or the one whose key
      answers the last value, the key read as `sort` reads it. The
      element itself is the answer, the first of equal ones, and the
      empty vector yields null.
  ~~|
  ::builtin{}

:vec/max/answersTheGreatest
  |~~ The greatest element answers. ~~|
  ::law~([3 1 4 1 5] | max | eq 5)

:vec/max/answersTheElementOfTheGreatestKey
  |~~ The element whose key answers most answers whole. ~~|
  ::law~([{:age 30} {:age 40}] | max ~(/age) | eq {:age 40})

:vec/max/ordersAcrossKinds
  |~~ A string follows a number in the one order. ~~|
  ::law~([3 "a" null] | max | eq "a")

:vec/max/key
  |~~ The key the call gives, null when it gives none. ~~|
  ~(at 0)

:vec/max/key/NotQuoteOrNullError
  |~~ The key is code, a quote or null. ~~|
  ::holds~(is ::quote ::null)

:vec/filter
  |~~ The elements whose predicate answers true, in their order. ~~|
  ::builtin{}

:vec/filter/keepsWhatThePredicateHolds
  |~~ The elements past two are kept in their order. ~~|
  ::law~([1 2 3 4 5] | filter ~(gt 2) | eq [3 4 5])

:vec/filter/readsEachElement
  |~~ The predicate reads each element as its subject. ~~|
  ::law~([{:age 25} {:age 15}] | filter ~(/age | gte 18) | eq [{:age 25}])

:vec/filter/takesAVerb
  |~~ A verb stands where a quote would. ~~|
  ::law~([1 -2 3] | :any/positive ~(gt 0) | filter ~positive | eq [1 3])

:vec/filter/predicate
  |~~ The code each element is run through, which answers a boolean.

      The predicate of `filter` is code it runs on each element, so it takes a
      quote, `filter ~(gt 1)`; a value computed at the call is refused.
  ~~|
  ~(/0)

:vec/filter/predicate/refusesABoolean
  |~~ A value computed at the call is refused, a boolean among them. ~~|
  ::law~([1 2 3] | filter (count | gt 1) !| [type /actualType] | eq [::vec/filter/predicate/NotQuoteError ::boolean])

:vec/filter/predicate/refusesAStepWithoutItsQuote
  |~~ A step written without its quote runs on the whole subject at the call, and the error it answers is refused alike. ~~|
  ::law~([{:a true}] | filter /a
    !| [type /actualType]
    |  eq [::vec/filter/predicate/NotQuoteError ::proj/IndexNotIntegerError])

:vec/filter/predicate/NotQuoteError
  |~~ The predicate is code, a quote. ~~|
  ::holds~(is ::quote)

:vec/filter/kept
  |~~ The elements the predicate accepted, in order. ~~|
  ::vec

:vec/filter/ConditionNotBooleanError
  |~~ The predicate of `filter` answers a boolean for each element;
      `:index` names the element whose predicate answered another kind.
  ~~|

:vec/filter/ConditionNotBooleanError/refusesANumberAnswer
  |~~ A predicate that answers a number is refused at the element. ~~|
  ::law~([1 2] | filter ~(1) !| [type /index /actualType] | eq [::vec/filter/ConditionNotBooleanError 0 ::number])

:vec/every
  |~~ Whether the predicate answers true for every element, the question
      a law asks of a listing; it stops at the first false.
  ~~|
  ::builtin{}

:vec/every/holdsOfEveryElement
  |~~ Every element is positive. ~~|
  ::law~([2 4 6 8] | every ~(gt 0) | eq true)

:vec/every/failsOnOneElement
  |~~ One element below the bar fails it. ~~|
  ::law~([1 2 3] | every ~(gt 2) | eq false)

:vec/every/takesAVerb
  |~~ A verb stands where a quote would. ~~|
  ::law~([2 4 6] | :any/positive ~(gt 0) | every ~positive | eq true)

:vec/every/holdsOfNothing
  |~~ An empty container answers true. ~~|
  ::law~([] | every ~(gt 0) | eq true)

:vec/every/predicate
  |~~ The code each element is run through, which answers a boolean.

      The predicate of `every` is code it runs on each element, so it takes a
      quote, `every ~(gt 0)`; a value computed at the call is refused.
  ~~|
  ~(/0)

:vec/every/predicate/refusesABoolean
  |~~ A value computed at the call is refused, a boolean among them. ~~|
  ::law~([1 2 3] | every (count | gt 1) !| [type /actualType] | eq [::vec/every/predicate/NotQuoteError ::boolean])

:vec/every/predicate/NotQuoteError
  |~~ The predicate is code, a quote. ~~|
  ::holds~(is ::quote)

:vec/every/all
  |~~ Whether the predicate held for every element. ~~|
  ::boolean

:vec/every/ConditionNotBooleanError
  |~~ The predicate of `every` answers a boolean for each element;
      `:index` names the element whose predicate answered another kind.
  ~~|

:vec/every/ConditionNotBooleanError/refusesANumberAnswer
  |~~ A predicate that answers a number is refused at the element. ~~|
  ::law~([1 2] | every ~(1) !| [type /index /actualType] | eq [::vec/every/ConditionNotBooleanError 0 ::number])

:vec/any
  |~~ Whether the predicate answers true for some element; it stops at
      the first true.
  ~~|
  ::builtin{}

:vec/any/holdsOfSomeElement
  |~~ One element past two is enough. ~~|
  ::law~([1 2 3] | any ~(gt 2) | eq true)

:vec/any/failsOfNone
  |~~ It is false when no element holds. ~~|
  ::law~([1 2 3] | any ~(gt 99) | eq false)

:vec/any/takesAVerb
  |~~ A verb stands where a quote would. ~~|
  ::law~([1 2 3] | :any/big ~(gt 2) | any ~big | eq true)

:vec/any/failsOfNothing
  |~~ An empty container answers false. ~~|
  ::law~([] | any ~(gt 0) | eq false)

:vec/any/predicate
  |~~ The code each element is run through, which answers a boolean.

      The predicate of `any` is code it runs on each element, so it takes a
      quote, `any ~(gt 2)`; a value computed at the call is refused.
  ~~|
  ~(/0)

:vec/any/predicate/refusesABoolean
  |~~ A value computed at the call is refused, a boolean among them. ~~|
  ::law~([1 2 3] | any (count | gt 1) !| [type /actualType] | eq [::vec/any/predicate/NotQuoteError ::boolean])

:vec/any/predicate/NotQuoteError
  |~~ The predicate is code, a quote. ~~|
  ::holds~(is ::quote)

:vec/any/some
  |~~ Whether the predicate held for some element. ~~|
  ::boolean

:vec/any/ConditionNotBooleanError
  |~~ The predicate of `any` answers a boolean for each element;
      `:index` names the element whose predicate answered another kind.
  ~~|

:vec/any/ConditionNotBooleanError/refusesANumberAnswer
  |~~ A predicate that answers a number is refused at the element. ~~|
  ::law~([1 2] | any ~(1) !| [type /index /actualType] | eq [::vec/any/ConditionNotBooleanError 0 ::number])

:vec/has
  |~~ Whether the vector holds the element, an element equal to it; the
      membership of a JSON array.
  ~~|
  ::builtin{}

:vec/has/findsAnElement
  |~~ An element held is found. ~~|
  ::law~(["ops" "db"] | has "ops" | eq true)

:vec/has/findsAnEqualVector
  |~~ A vector element is found by equality. ~~|
  ::law~([1 [2]] | has [2] | eq true)

:vec/has/element
  |~~ The element the call gives. ~~|
  ~(/0)

:vec/has/held
  |~~ Whether the vector holds the element. ~~|
  ::boolean

:vec/contains
  |~~ Whether the vector holds an element equal to the value, the
      membership `has` asks, under the word a string answers for its
      substrings.
  ~~|
  ::builtin{}

:vec/contains/findsAnElement
  |~~ An element held is found. ~~|
  ::law~(["ops" "db"] | contains "ops" | eq true)

:vec/contains/element
  |~~ The element the call gives. ~~|
  ~(/0)

:vec/contains/found
  |~~ Whether the vector holds the element. ~~|
  ::boolean

:vec/groupBy
  |~~ The map of vectors the key sorts the elements into. A key of a
      map is a keyword, and a key that answers a string, the field of a
      JSON object, or a number files its element under the keyword of
      its text.
  ~~|
  ::builtin{}

:vec/groupBy/filesUnderTheKey
  |~~ The elements file under the key they answer. ~~|
  ::law~([{:k :a :v 1} {:k :b :v 2} {:k :a :v 3}] | groupBy ~(/k) | /a * /v | eq [1 3])

:vec/groupBy/filesAStringUnderItsKeyword
  |~~ A string key files under its keyword. ~~|
  ::law~([{:s "paid"} {:s "open"} {:s "paid"}] | groupBy ~(/s) * count | eq {:paid 2 :open 1})

:vec/groupBy/filesUnderItselfWithoutAKey
  |~~ Without a key an element files under itself, so the occurrences of each value are counted by `groupBy * count`. ~~|
  ::law~(["b" "a" "b"] | groupBy * count | eq {:b 2 :a 1})

:vec/groupBy/keepsTheOrderInAGroup
  |~~ A group keeps its elements in their order. ~~|
  ::law~([{:dept :eng :name "a"} {:dept :sales :name "b"} {:dept :eng :name "c"}] | groupBy ~(/dept) | /eng * /name | eq ["a" "c"])

:vec/groupBy/filesANumberUnderItsText
  |~~ A number key files under the keyword of its text. ~~|
  ::law~([{:k 1} {:k 2} {:k 1}] | groupBy ~(/k) | keys | eq #[:"1" :"2"])

:vec/groupBy/key
  |~~ The key the call gives, null when it gives none.

      The key of `groupBy` is code it runs on each element, so it takes a
      quote, `groupBy ~(/kind)`; a value computed at the call is refused.
  ~~|
  ~(at 0)

:vec/groupBy/key/refusesAKeyword
  |~~ A keyword given as the key is computed at the call, and refused. ~~|
  ::law~([{:kind :a}] | groupBy :a !| [type /actualType] | eq [::vec/groupBy/key/NotQuoteOrNullError ::keyword])

:vec/groupBy/key/NotQuoteOrNullError
  |~~ The key is code, a quote or null. ~~|
  ::holds~(is ::quote ::null)

:vec/groupBy/groups
  |~~ The elements filed under the keyword their key answered. ~~|
  ::map

:vec/groupBy/KeyNotKeywordError
  |~~ The key `groupBy` files an element under is a keyword, or a string
      or a number, filed under the keyword of its text, since the keys of
      a map are keywords; any other value is refused, and `:index` names
      the element.
  ~~|

:vec/groupBy/KeyNotKeywordError/refusesABooleanKey
  |~~ A boolean key is refused at its element. ~~|
  ::law~([{:k true} {:k 2}] | groupBy ~(/k) !| [type /actualType /index] | eq [::vec/groupBy/KeyNotKeywordError ::boolean 0])

:vec/indexBy
  |~~ The map of the elements under the key each answers, the last
      element winning a key two share; a key that answers a string is
      made a keyword first, as for `groupBy`.
  ~~|
  ::builtin{}

:vec/indexBy/filesEachUnderItsKey
  |~~ Each element files under its key. ~~|
  ::law~([{:id :a :n 1} {:id :b :n 2}] | indexBy ~(/id) | /b/n | eq 2)

:vec/indexBy/filesAStringUnderItself
  |~~ Without a key a string files under its keyword. ~~|
  ::law~(["a" "b"] | indexBy | eq {:a "a" :b "b"})

:vec/indexBy/readsAFieldOfAnEntry
  |~~ An entry is read by its key and then by a field. ~~|
  ::law~([{:id :a :name "alice"} {:id :b :name "bob"}] | indexBy ~(/id) | /a/name | eq "alice")

:vec/indexBy/filesANumberUnderItsText
  |~~ A number key files under the keyword of its text. ~~|
  ::law~([{:id 7 :name "ann"}] | indexBy ~(/id) | /7/name | eq "ann")

:vec/indexBy/filesAKeywordUnderItself
  |~~ Without a key a keyword files under itself. ~~|
  ::law~([:x :y] | indexBy | eq {:x :x :y :y})

:vec/indexBy/key
  |~~ The key the call gives, null when it gives none.

      The key of `indexBy` is code it runs on each element, so it takes a
      quote, `indexBy ~(/id)`; a value computed at the call is refused.
  ~~|
  ~(at 0)

:vec/indexBy/key/refusesAKeyword
  |~~ A keyword given as the key is computed at the call, and refused. ~~|
  ::law~([{:id :a}] | indexBy :a !| [type /actualType] | eq [::vec/indexBy/key/NotQuoteOrNullError ::keyword])

:vec/indexBy/key/NotQuoteOrNullError
  |~~ The key is code, a quote or null. ~~|
  ::holds~(is ::quote ::null)

:vec/indexBy/index
  |~~ Each element under the keyword its key answered, the last of alike ones. ~~|
  ::map

:vec/indexBy/KeyNotKeywordError
  |~~ The key `indexBy` files an element under is a keyword, or a string
      or a number, filed under the keyword of its text, since the keys of
      a map are keywords; any other value is refused, and `:index` names
      the element.
  ~~|

:vec/indexBy/KeyNotKeywordError/refusesAVectorKey
  |~~ A vector key is refused at its element. ~~|
  ::law~([{:k [1]}] | indexBy ~(/k) !| [type /actualType /index] | eq [::vec/indexBy/KeyNotKeywordError ::vec 0])

:vec/sort
  |~~ The elements in the one order of values, the order every value
      takes against every other, the way a reader ranks what a query
      found. `sort ~(key)` ranks by the value the key answers for each
      element, a vector it answers ranking place by place, so `sort
      ~([(/count | desc) /name])` reads as `ORDER BY count DESC, name`;
      elements the key leaves alike keep the order of the subject.

      Kinds come first, null, boolean, number, string, keyword, tag
      name, vector, set, map, quote, doc, error, elision and desc, then
      every other tag by its name.
  ~~|
  ::builtin{}

:vec/sort/ordersNumbers
  |~~ Numbers ascend, equal ones side by side. ~~|
  ::law~([3 1 4 1 5] | sort | eq [1 1 3 4 5])

:vec/sort/ordersByTheKey
  |~~ A key orders by what it answers for each element. ~~|
  ::law~([{:age 30} {:age 20}] | sort ~(/age) | eq [{:age 20} {:age 30}])

:vec/sort/descendsUnderDesc
  |~~ A key under `desc` descends, and a vector key breaks the ties by its next place. ~~|
  ::law~([{:age 30} {:age 40}] | sort ~(/age | desc) | first | /age | eq 40)

:vec/sort/breaksTiesByTheNextPlace
  |~~ A vector key breaks the ties of its first place by its next. ~~|
  ::law~([{:n 1 :s "b"} {:n 2 :s "c"} {:n 2 :s "a"}] | sort ~([(/n | desc) /s]) * /s | eq ["a" "c" "b"])

:vec/sort/ordersKindsFirst
  |~~ Null comes before numbers, and numbers before strings. ~~|
  ::law~([3 null "x" 1] | sort | eq [null 1 3 "x"])

:vec/sort/ordersTagsByName
  |~~ Tag names order by their names. ~~|
  ::law~([::B ::A] | sort | eq [::A ::B])

:vec/sort/ranksByTheFirstPlace
  |~~ A vector key ranks by its first place, then by the next among the elements the first leaves alike. ~~|
  ::law~([{:a 1 :b 2} {:a 1 :b 1} {:a 0 :b 9}] | sort ~([/a /b]) * /b | eq [9 1 2])

:vec/sort/putsNullLast
  |~~ A first place of `eq null` puts null last. ~~|
  ::law~([3 null 1] | sort ~([(eq null) /]) | eq [1 3 null])

:vec/sort/turnsTheKey
  |~~ `desc` turns the key, or one place of it, to descend. ~~|
  ::law~([{:k 1} {:k 2}] | sort ~(/k | desc) | eq [{:k 2} {:k 1}])

:vec/sort/refusesAKeyThatMissesItsField
  |~~ A key that answers an error answers the call, a place of a vector it answers among them, so a key that misses its field is refused where it is read. ~~|
  ::law~([{:a 2} {:b 1}] | sort ~(/a) !| type | eq ::proj/KeyNotInMapError)

:vec/sort/refusesAPlaceThatMissesItsField
  |~~ A place of a vector key that misses its field is refused alike. ~~|
  ::law~([{:a 2} {:b 1}] | sort ~([/a]) !| type | eq ::proj/KeyNotInMapError)

:vec/sort/key
  |~~ The key the call gives, null when it gives none.

      The key of `sort` is code it runs on each element, so it takes a
      quote, `sort ~(/age)`; a value computed at the call is refused.
  ~~|
  ~(at 0)

:vec/sort/key/refusesANumber
  |~~ A number given as the key is computed at the call, and refused. ~~|
  ::law~([3 1 2] | sort 1 !| [type /actualType] | eq [::vec/sort/key/NotQuoteOrNullError ::number])

:vec/sort/key/NotQuoteOrNullError
  |~~ The key is code, a quote or null. ~~|
  ::holds~(is ::quote ::null)

:vec/sort/sorted
  |~~ The elements in the order the key ranks them. ~~|
  ::vec

:vec/take
  |~~ The first count elements. The count is a whole number: past the
      length the whole vector comes back, and a negative count takes
      nothing.
  ~~|
  ::builtin{}

:vec/take/takesTheFirst
  |~~ Three of five are the first three. ~~|
  ::law~([1 2 3 4 5] | take 3 | eq [1 2 3])

:vec/take/takesNothingByANegativeCount
  |~~ A negative count takes nothing. ~~|
  ::law~([1 2 3] | take -1 | eq [])

:vec/take/count
  |~~ How many. ~~|
  ~(/0)

:vec/take/count/NotNumberError
  |~~ The count is a number. ~~|
  ::holds~(is ::number)

:vec/take/count/NotIntegerError
  |~~ The count is a whole number. ~~|
  ::holds~(eq (round))

:vec/take/taken
  |~~ The first elements. ~~|
  ::vec

:vec/drop
  |~~ The elements after the first count. The count is a whole number:
      past the length the empty vector comes back, and a negative count
      drops nothing.
  ~~|
  ::builtin{}

:vec/drop/dropsTheFirst
  |~~ Dropping two leaves the last three. ~~|
  ::law~([1 2 3 4 5] | drop 2 | eq [3 4 5])

:vec/drop/dropsNothingByANegativeCount
  |~~ A negative count drops nothing. ~~|
  ::law~([1 2 3] | drop -1 | eq [1 2 3])

:vec/drop/count
  |~~ How many. ~~|
  ~(/0)

:vec/drop/count/NotNumberError
  |~~ The count is a number. ~~|
  ::holds~(is ::number)

:vec/drop/count/NotIntegerError
  |~~ The count is a whole number. ~~|
  ::holds~(eq (round))

:vec/drop/rest
  |~~ The elements after the ones dropped. ~~|
  ::vec

:vec/at
  |~~ The element at the index, a negative one counting from the end,
      or past either end the default the call gives, null when it gives
      none; an element that is null stays null.
  ~~|
  ::builtin{}

:vec/at/readsTheIndex
  |~~ Index one is the second element. ~~|
  ::law~([10 20 30] | at 1 | eq 20)

:vec/at/countsFromTheEnd
  |~~ A negative index counts from the end. ~~|
  ::law~([10 20 30] | at -1 | eq 30)

:vec/at/answersTheDefaultPastTheEnd
  |~~ Past the end the default answers. ~~|
  ::law~([] | at 0 7 | eq 7)

:vec/at/keepsANullElement
  |~~ An element that is null answers null, not the default. ~~|
  ::law~([null] | at 0 7 | eq null)

:vec/at/answersNullPastTheEnd
  |~~ Past the end without a default null answers. ~~|
  ::law~([10 20 30] | at 99 | eq null)

:vec/at/default
  |~~ The value answered for a place the subject does not hold, null when it gives none. ~~|
  ~(at 1)

:vec/at/index
  |~~ The index, a negative one counting from the end. ~~|
  ~(/0)

:vec/at/index/NotNumberError
  |~~ The index is a number. ~~|
  ::holds~(is ::number)

:vec/at/index/NotIntegerError
  |~~ The index is a whole number. ~~|
  ::holds~(eq (round))

:vec/distinct
  |~~ The set of the elements, the vector in the one order without
      duplicates. Two elements are one when `eq` says so, so two maps,
      vectors or sets of the same content collapse.
  ~~|
  ::builtin{}

:vec/distinct/answersASet
  |~~ The distinct elements are a set. ~~|
  ::law~([2 1 2 3 1] | distinct | eq #[1 2 3])

:vec/distinct/ordersByTheOneOrder
  |~~ The set's vector is in the one order. ~~|
  ::law~([2 1 2 3 1] | distinct | payload | eq [1 2 3])

:vec/distinct/mergesEqualMaps
  |~~ Two maps of the same entries are one. ~~|
  ::law~([{:id 1} {:id 2} {:id 1}] | distinct | eq #[{:id 1} {:id 2}])

:vec/distinct/unique
  |~~ The elements, each once, in the one order: a set. ~~|
  ::vec

:vec/reverse
  |~~ The elements in the reverse order. ~~|
  ::builtin{}

:vec/reverse/reversesTheOrder
  |~~ The elements come in reverse order. ~~|
  ::law~([1 2 3] | reverse | eq [3 2 1])

:vec/reverse/reversed
  |~~ The elements in reverse order. ~~|
  ::vec

:vec/flat
  |~~ The elements with one level of nesting spliced in. ~~|
  ::builtin{}

:vec/flat/splicesOneLevel
  |~~ The inner vectors splice into one. ~~|
  ::law~([[1 2] [3] [4 5]] | flat | eq [1 2 3 4 5])

:vec/flat/flattened
  |~~ The elements with one level of nesting spliced in. ~~|
  ::vec

:vec/reduce
  |~~ The seed folded over the elements by the reducer, which runs as
      `accumulator | reducer element`; `sum`, `count`, `max` and the
      builders of structures all factor through it.
  ~~|
  ::builtin{}

:vec/reduce/sums
  |~~ Adding from zero sums. ~~|
  ::law~([1 2 3 4 5] | reduce 0 ~(add) | eq 15)

:vec/reduce/multiplies
  |~~ Multiplying from one gives the product. ~~|
  ::law~([1 2 3 4 5] | reduce 1 ~(mul) | eq 120)

:vec/reduce/joinsStrings
  |~~ Appending from the empty string joins. ~~|
  ::law~(["a" "b" "c"] | reduce "" ~(append) | eq "abc")

:vec/reduce/unitesSets
  |~~ Uniting from the empty set merges. ~~|
  ::law~([#[1] #[2 3]] | reduce #[] ~(union) | eq #[1 2 3])

:vec/reduce/takesAName
  |~~ A name takes the accumulator as its subject and the element as its first arg. ~~|
  ::law~(:number/max2 ~(if (gt x) ~(/) ~(x)) | :number/max2/x |~~ The element. ~~| ~(/0) | :number/max2/x/NotNumberError ::holds~(is ::number) | [3 1 4 1 5] | reduce 0 ~max2 | eq 5)

:vec/reduce/takesANameThatLeavesTheElement
  |~~ A name may leave the element unread. ~~|
  ::law~(:any/tally ~(add 1) | :any/tally/el |~~ The element, unread. ~~| ~(/0) | [10 20 30] | reduce 0 ~tally | eq 3)

:vec/reduce/answersTheSeedOfNothing
  |~~ The seed is the first accumulator, answered as it is for an empty subject. ~~|
  ::law~([] | reduce 42 ~(add) | eq 42)

:vec/reduce/reducer
  |~~ The code that folds an element into the value so far.

      The reducer of `reduce` is code it runs on the accumulator with each element, so it takes a
      quote, `reduce 0 ~(add)`; a value computed at the call is refused.
  ~~|
  ~(/1)

:vec/reduce/reducer/refusesANumber
  |~~ A number computed at the call is refused. ~~|
  ::law~([1 2 3] | reduce 0 1 !| [type /actualType] | eq [::vec/reduce/reducer/NotQuoteError ::number])

:vec/reduce/reducer/NotQuoteError
  |~~ The reducer is code, a quote. ~~|
  ::holds~(is ::quote)

:vec/reduce/seed
  |~~ The value the fold starts from. ~~|
  ~(/0)

:vec/reduce/ReducerNotBinaryError
  |~~ The quote of `reduce` names one verb, which the fold calls with the
      accumulator as its subject and the element as its first arg,
      `reduce 0 ~(add)`; a quote of several steps, a literal or a
      name of no verb is refused. A fold of several steps is a name
      declared first, whose part names the element.
  ~~|

:vec/reduce/ReducerNotBinaryError/refusesAPipeline
  |~~ A reducer of two steps is refused. ~~|
  ::law~([1 2 3] | reduce 0 ~(mul 2 | add) !| type | eq ::vec/reduce/ReducerNotBinaryError)

:vec/reduce/ReducerNotBinaryError/takesANameForAPipeline
  |~~ A pipeline under a name of its own folds as one verb. ~~|
  ::law~(:any/step ~(mul 2 | add el) | :any/step/el ~(/0) | :any/step/el/NotNumberError ::holds~(is ::number)
    | [1 2 3] | reduce 0 ~(step)
    | eq 11)

:vec/union
  |~~ The union of the operands the vector holds, sets merged in the one
      order or maps merged with the last value winning a key. A vector is
      no set for the algebra, so another operand beside it is
      refused with the pair.
  ~~|
  ::builtin{}

:vec/union/unitesTheOperands
  |~~ The sets the vector holds unite. ~~|
  ::law~([#[:a :b] #[:b :c]] | union | eq #[:a :b :c])

:vec/union/refusesAnOperandBeside
  |~~ An operand beside the vector is refused with the pair. ~~|
  ::law~([1] | union #[2] !| type | eq ::vec/union/OperandsMismatchedError)

:vec/union/with
  |~~ The value the subject is joined with, null when it gives none. ~~|
  ~(at 0)

:vec/union/with/NotSetOrMapOrNullError
  |~~ The with is a set, a map or null. ~~|
  ::holds~(is ::set ::map ::null)

:vec/union/NoOperandsError
  |~~ `union` over a vector joins its operands from the first, `[a b c]
      | union`, so the vector holds one at least; two operands are
      written `a | union b`.
  ~~|

:vec/union/NoOperandsError/refusesTheEmptyVector
  |~~ The empty vector holds no operand. ~~|
  ::law~([] | union !| type | eq ::vec/union/NoOperandsError)

:vec/union/OperandsMismatchedError
  |~~ The operands `union` joins are two sets or two maps; a vector is
      none, so an operand its call gives beside the vector is refused with
      the pair.
  ~~|

:vec/union/OperandsMismatchedError/refusesASetAndAMap
  |~~ A set and a map do not join. ~~|
  ::law~([#[:a] {:b 1}] | union !| [type /leftType /rightType] | eq [::vec/union/OperandsMismatchedError ::set ::map])

:vec/union/OperandsMismatchedError/refusesAVectorAndASet
  |~~ A vector and a set do not join. ~~|
  ::law~([1] | union #[2] !| [type /leftType /rightType] | eq [::vec/union/OperandsMismatchedError ::vec ::set])

:vec/union/OperandsMismatchedError/leftType
  |~~ The kind of the operand on the left. ~~|
  ::tag

:vec/union/OperandsMismatchedError/rightType
  |~~ The kind of the operand on the right. ~~|
  ::tag

:vec/minus
  |~~ The first operand the vector holds minus each one after it; another
      operand beside the vector is refused with the pair.
  ~~|
  ::builtin{}

:vec/minus/subtractsTheRest
  |~~ The first set loses what the next holds. ~~|
  ::law~([#[:a :b :c] #[:b]] | minus | eq #[:a :c])

:vec/minus/with
  |~~ The value the subject is joined with, null when it gives none. ~~|
  ~(at 0)

:vec/minus/with/NotSetOrMapOrNullError
  |~~ The with is a set, a map or null. ~~|
  ::holds~(is ::set ::map ::null)

:vec/minus/NoOperandsError
  |~~ `minus` over a vector joins its operands from the first, `[a b c]
      | minus`, so the vector holds one at least; two operands are
      written `a | minus b`.
  ~~|

:vec/minus/NoOperandsError/refusesTheEmptyVector
  |~~ The empty vector holds no operand. ~~|
  ::law~([] | minus !| type | eq ::vec/minus/NoOperandsError)

:vec/minus/OperandsMismatchedError
  |~~ The operands `minus` joins are two sets, or a map and a map or a
      vector of keys; a vector is none, so an operand its call gives beside
      the vector is refused with the pair.
  ~~|

:vec/minus/OperandsMismatchedError/refusesASetAndAMap
  |~~ A set and a map do not join. ~~|
  ::law~([#[:a] {:b 1}] | minus !| [type /leftType /rightType] | eq [::vec/minus/OperandsMismatchedError ::set ::map])

:vec/minus/OperandsMismatchedError/refusesAVectorAndASet
  |~~ A vector and a set do not join. ~~|
  ::law~([1] | minus #[2] !| [type /leftType /rightType] | eq [::vec/minus/OperandsMismatchedError ::vec ::set])

:vec/minus/OperandsMismatchedError/leftType
  |~~ The kind of the operand on the left. ~~|
  ::tag

:vec/minus/OperandsMismatchedError/rightType
  |~~ The kind of the operand on the right. ~~|
  ::tag

:vec/minus/KeyNotKeywordOrStringError
  |~~ A key `minus` takes out of a map is a keyword or a string; `:index`
      names the first element of the vector of keys that is neither.
  ~~|

:vec/minus/KeyNotKeywordOrStringError/refusesANumber
  |~~ A number among the keys is refused at its index. ~~|
  ::law~([{:a 1} [:a 1]] | minus !| [type /actualType /index] | eq [::vec/minus/KeyNotKeywordOrStringError ::number 1])

:vec/inter
  |~~ The intersection of the operands the vector holds; another operand
      beside the vector is refused with the pair.
  ~~|
  ::builtin{}

:vec/inter/keepsTheCommonElements
  |~~ The elements both sets hold stay. ~~|
  ::law~([#[:a :b :c] #[:b :d]] | inter | eq #[:b])

:vec/inter/with
  |~~ The value the subject is joined with, null when it gives none. ~~|
  ~(at 0)

:vec/inter/with/NotSetOrMapOrNullError
  |~~ The with is a set, a map or null. ~~|
  ::holds~(is ::set ::map ::null)

:vec/inter/NoOperandsError
  |~~ `inter` over a vector joins its operands from the first, `[a b c]
      | inter`, so the vector holds one at least; two operands are
      written `a | inter b`.
  ~~|

:vec/inter/NoOperandsError/refusesTheEmptyVector
  |~~ The empty vector holds no operand. ~~|
  ::law~([] | inter !| type | eq ::vec/inter/NoOperandsError)

:vec/inter/OperandsMismatchedError
  |~~ The operands `inter` joins are two sets, or a map and a map or a
      vector of keys; a vector is none, so an operand its call gives beside
      the vector is refused with the pair.
  ~~|

:vec/inter/OperandsMismatchedError/refusesASetAndAMap
  |~~ A set and a map do not join. ~~|
  ::law~([#[:a] {:b 1}] | inter !| [type /leftType /rightType] | eq [::vec/inter/OperandsMismatchedError ::set ::map])

:vec/inter/OperandsMismatchedError/refusesAVectorAndASet
  |~~ A vector and a set do not join. ~~|
  ::law~([1] | inter #[2] !| [type /leftType /rightType] | eq [::vec/inter/OperandsMismatchedError ::vec ::set])

:vec/inter/OperandsMismatchedError/leftType
  |~~ The kind of the operand on the left. ~~|
  ::tag

:vec/inter/OperandsMismatchedError/rightType
  |~~ The kind of the operand on the right. ~~|
  ::tag

:vec/inter/KeyNotKeywordOrStringError
  |~~ A key `inter` keeps of a map is a keyword or a string; `:index`
      names the first element of the vector of keys that is neither.
  ~~|

:vec/inter/KeyNotKeywordOrStringError/refusesAVector
  |~~ A vector among the keys is refused at its index. ~~|
  ::law~([{:a 1} [[:a]]] | inter !| [type /actualType /index] | eq [::vec/inter/KeyNotKeywordOrStringError ::vec 0])

:vec/join
  |~~ The strings the vector holds joined with the separator between
      consecutive ones. Inverse of `split`.

      The subject of `join` is the vector of strings it writes as one; a
      map's values are read first, `vals | join ","`.
  ~~|
  ::builtin{}

:vec/join/joinsWithTheSeparator
  |~~ The separator stands between the strings. ~~|
  ::law~(["a" "b" "c"] | join "," | eq "a,b,c")

:vec/join/joinsNothingToTheEmptyString
  |~~ The empty vector joins to the empty string. ~~|
  ::law~([] | join "," | eq "")

:vec/join/refusesANumber
  |~~ A number is no vector of strings. ~~|
  ::law~(42 | join "," !| [type /actualType] | eq [::vec/join/SubjectNotVecError ::number])

:vec/join/separator
  |~~ The separator the call gives.

      The separator of `join` is a string, `join ", "`.
  ~~|
  ~(/0)

:vec/join/separator/refusesANumber
  |~~ A number separator is refused. ~~|
  ::law~(["a" "b"] | join 42 !| [type /actualType] | eq [::vec/join/separator/NotStringError ::number])

:vec/join/separator/NotStringError
  |~~ The separator is a string. ~~|
  ::holds~(is ::string)

:vec/join/joined
  |~~ The elements joined into one string by the separator. ~~|
  ::string

:vec/join/ElementNotStringError
  |~~ Every element `join` writes is a string; `:index` names the first
      that is not, and `* print` makes each value its text first.
  ~~|

:vec/join/ElementNotStringError/refusesANumber
  |~~ A number among the strings is refused at its index. ~~|
  ::law~(["a" 42 "c"] | join "," !| [type /actualType /index] | eq [::vec/join/ElementNotStringError ::number 1])

:vec/prepend
  |~~ The vector with the element at its head, any value; a set takes it
      through its constructor, so it stays a set.
  ~~|
  ::builtin{}

:vec/prepend/putsTheElementFirst
  |~~ The element goes first. ~~|
  ::law~([2 3] | prepend 1 | eq [1 2 3])

:vec/prepend/keepsASetASet
  |~~ A set takes the element as a set does. ~~|
  ::law~(#[2 3] | prepend 3 | eq #[2 3])

:vec/prepend/element
  |~~ The element the call gives. ~~|
  ~(/0)

:vec/prepend/prepended
  |~~ The vector with the value as its first element. ~~|
  ::vec

:vec/append
  |~~ The vector with the element at its end, any value, so `[1] |
      append [2 3]` answers `[1 [2 3]]` and vectors join through
      `flat`; a set takes it through its constructor, so it stays a set.
  ~~|
  ::builtin{}

:vec/append/putsTheElementLast
  |~~ The element goes last. ~~|
  ::law~([1 2] | append 3 | eq [1 2 3])

:vec/append/keepsASetASet
  |~~ A set takes the element as a set does. ~~|
  ::law~(#[1 2] | append 1 | eq #[1 2])

:vec/append/element
  |~~ The element the call gives. ~~|
  ~(/0)

:vec/append/appended
  |~~ The vector with the value as its last element. ~~|
  ::vec
